# ⚗️ Molecule Mischief: Chemistry meets Maths

An interactive gas-law lab where random particles turn into a perfect probability curve.
Built as a prototype for the [Tenali](https://github.com/vicharanashala/tenali) project.

**🔗 Live demo:** https://<curious-chemist>.github.io/<REPO-NAME>/

## What it shows
- 🎈 Live particle simulation (2D ideal gas) with temperature, volume and particle-count sliders
- 📊 Speed histogram vs the **Maxwell–Boltzmann** curve `f(v) = (v/kT)·e^(−v²/2kT)`
- 🧮 Measured pressure (from wall impacts) vs predicted `P·A = N·kT`
- 📈 Boyle's Law recorder, 🎯 target-pressure challenge, 🏅 badges, 📝 quiz
- 🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Characters: Chilly, Zippy and Prof. Tenali explain what is happening

## Maths and chemistry concepts
Probability density, area under a curve = 1, integration (mean speed), differentiation (peak speed),
square-root scaling, Ideal Gas Law, Boyle's law, temperature and molecular motion.

## Project structure
```
index.html                 full standalone website (open directly in a browser)
src/ChemMathsLab.jsx       React version of the simulation (for the Tenali app)
docs/CHEM_MATHS_LAB.md     concept and integration notes
```

## Run locally
Just open `index.html` in any browser. No install needed.

## Model notes
2D gas, unit mass, no particle–particle collisions. Temperature change rescales velocities by √(T_new/T_old).
Pressure = total wall impulse / (time × perimeter).

